  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rational\nThe main bottleneck in the current approach is its reliance on gradient-based optimization with a smoothing technique, which tends to get stuck in local optima. Here, we introduce a multi-phase optimization strategy combining: (1) CMA-ES evolutionary optimization which explores a broader search space, (2) local gradient optimization that refines solutions, and (3) multi-resolution initialization using function families. This hybrid approach addresses the non-convex nature of the problem.\n\nWe rationalize that the core issue is the difficulty in finding the globally optimal function h(x). The proposed method systematically explores the function space at multiple resolutions, transferring successful patterns between scales, and uses derivative-free and derivative-based methods synergistically.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nDefine h(x) as a piecewise constant function on [0,2] with:\n- Integral = 1\n- Values in [0,1]\n- Free-form otherwise\n\nThe objective to minimize is the maximum correlation:\nC5 = (2.0/n) * max_k \u2211_{i} h[i] * (1 - h[i+k])\n\nWhere:\n- Mode = 'full' for correlation computation\n- k ranges over all lags from -(n-1) to (n-1)\n- dx = 2.0/n\n\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = n/2, and n is the resolution.\n\nKey operations:\n- Initialization: Interpolate/scale base functions from known extremal families\n- Evolution: Covariance matrix adaptation evolution strategy (CMA-ES)\n- Optimization: Projected gradient descent with adaptive line search\n- Repair: Projection to the feasible set [0,1]n with unit sum\n- Evaluation: Compute full correlation + maximum\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nPhase 1: Multi-resolution Initialization (5 minutes)\n- Sample four base function families at multiple scales:\n  1. Standard solution-like (two positive impulses normalized to sum 1)\n  2. Grid-aligned (cells covering half the domain)\n  3. Randomly sampled points (as fallback)\n  4. Monotonic sawtooth waves\n- Combine these into candidate functions at base resolution n0=100 and two supersets (200,400)\n\nPhase 2: Evolution Optimization (40% budget):\n- Use CMA-ES implementation from scratch to optimize base resolution\n- Toggle constraints management for nearly feasible points\n- Repair projections to enforce 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n- Track best candidate across generations\n\nPhase 3: Local Refinement (20% budget):\n- Apply tangent to\u6700\u4f18\u89e3\n- Run projected gradient optimization for refinement\n- Use adaptive step size and early termination conditions\n\nPhase 4: Resolution Adjustment (30% budget):\n- Transfer best patterns between resolutions\n- Use initial_Scalar interpolation to scale between 100, 200, and 400\n- For each new resolution:\n  - Project to unit-sum box\n  - Run short optimization session\n  - Compare best candidates across scales\n\nPhase 5: Final Selection (5% budget):\n- From all candidates, retain best one satisfying constraints\n- Force-box projection to ensure numerical stability\n- Return (h_vector, C5_bound, n_points)\n\n4. Search Choices and Use Variations\nWe govern the search with:\n\n- Initial population: Four base function families, varying scale and random components\n- Evolutionary parameters:\n  - nEs = 20 (number of evolution strategies)\n  - \u03c3 = 0.5 (initial standard deviation)\n  - Max evaluations = n_points * 2000 / budget\n- Local refinement hyperparameters:\n  - Step size factor = 0.001\n  - Min iterations = 100\n  - Early termination if improvement < 1e-5 for 20 steps\n- Multi-resolution strategy:\n  - Target resolutions: 100, 200, 400\n  - Scale factors: 2.0, 1.5 (non-integer scale for sawtooth)\n  - Optimization budget: 5 minutes per resolution upgrade\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nHigh-level budget allocation (1100 seconds):\n- Initial setup: 30 seconds\n- Multi-resolution init: 5 minutes\n- Evolution optimization: 400 seconds (prioritized)\n- Local refinement: 100 seconds\n- Resolution adjustment: 360 seconds\n- Final cleanup: 40 seconds\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Handle NaN/Inf in correlation calculations\n- Fallback to random initialization if initial setup fails\n- Timeout evolution if taking too long per generation\n- Monitor for feasibility during local refinement\n- If numerical underflow, use simple gradient approximation module\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nFeasibility checks for final candidate:\n0.0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1.0 for all i\nnp.isfinite(C5)\nsum(h) \u2248 n/2 (allowance \u00b10.5)\n\nFinal checks:\nCompute C5 value with original estimator\nEnsure function satisfies evaluator requirements\nReturn with:\n- Optimized h_values (length n_points)\n- C5_bound (minimized C5 value)\n- n_points (integer)\n\nHandles error propagation/equality conditions transparently:\n\ndef is_feasible(h):\n    if not np.logical_and(0 <= h, h <= 1).all(): return False\n    return True, np.isfinite(sum(h))\n\nFails gracefully for user-supplied constraints.\n\nThe implementation requires modifications in:\ngradient calculation logic\nprojection function\nbudget/iterative scheme\nbase function initialization\nCMA-ES implementation",
